  "text": "umably, the problem is to find the sum of all positive integers \\( n \\) such that \\( n + 2 \\) divides the product \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\nWe start by considering the divisibility condition:\n\\[ n + 2 \\mid 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\]\n\nThis means that \\( n + 2 \\) must divide the entire expression \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\). To proceed, we can examine the behavior of \\( n + 2 \\) for different values of \\( n \\). Let's test small positive integers for \\( n \\):\n\n1. For \\( n = 1 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 3 \\]\n   \\[ 3(1 + 3)(1^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 10 = 120 \\]\n   Since \\( 3 \\mid 120 \\), \\( n = 1 \\) is a solution.\n\n2. For \\( n = 2 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 4 \\]\n   \\[ 3(2 + 3)(2^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 13 = 195 \\]\n   Since \\( 4 \\nmid 195 \\), \\( n = 2 \\) is not a solution.\n\n3. For \\( n = 3 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 5 \\]\n   \\[ 3(3 + 3)(3^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 18 = 324 \\]\n   Since \\( 5 \\nmid 324 \\), \\( n = 3 \\) is not a solution.\n\n4. For \\( n = 4 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 6 \\]\n   \\[ 3(4 + 3)(4^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 25 = 525 \\]\n   Since \\( 6 \\nmid 525 \\), \\( n = 4 \\) is not a solution.\n\n5. For \\( n = 5 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 7 \\]\n   \\[ 3(5 + 3)(5^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 34 = 816 \\]\n   Since \\( 7 \\nmid 816 \\), \\( n = 5 \\) is not a solution.\n\n6. For \\( n = 6 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 8 \\]\n   \\[ 3(6 + 3)(6^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 45 = 1215 \\]\n   Since \\( 8 \\nmid 1215 \\), \\( n = 6 \\) is not a solution.\n\n7. For \\( n = 7 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 9 \\]\n   \\[ 3(7 + 3)(7^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 58 = 1740 \\]\n   Since \\( 9 \\nmid 1740 \\), \\( n = 7 \\) is not a solution.\n\n8. For \\( n = 8 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 10 \\]\n   \\[ 3(8 + 3)(8^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 73 = 2349 \\]\n   Since \\( 10 \\nmid 2349 \\), \\( n = 8 \\) is not a solution.\n\n9. For \\( n = 9 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 11 \\]\n   \\[ 3(9 + 3)(9^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 90 = 3240 \\]\n   Since \\( 11 \\nmid 3240 \\), \\( n = 9 \\) is not a solution.\n\n10. For \\( n = 10 \\):\n    \\[ n + 2 = 12 \\]\n    \\[ 3(10 + 3)(10^2 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 109 = 4185 \\]\n    Since \\( 12 \\nmid 4185 \\), \\( n = 10 \\) is not a solution.\n\nWe observe that for \\( n \\geq 2 \\), \\( n + 2 \\) does not divide \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\). Therefore, the only solution is \\( n = 1 \\).\n\nThe sum of all positive integers \\( n \\) that satisfy the condition is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
